I'm going to ramble a bit here. What else is new?

"John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:

> I've actually considered weakening unicorn horns
> for quite some time, even if the OP didn't.  I
> have yet to find anything it would break.

Let's start with that you'd break that wonderful
feeling the less skilled player has at _finally_
acquiring some protection against a whole slew of
early game tactical problems, a shift of issues that
allows concentration on the later, more strategic
parts of the game.

So far as I know, in usual game design, there are
_always_ mechanisms included to shift the problems
the player faces _in kind_ from one to another as
the game progresses, lest the boredom of always
worrying about one kind of issue throughout the game
ruin the game's playability.

        Zork is a very different game, once you
        acquire the lamp. Up until then, all you can
        do is worry about the Grue.

The unicorn horn is one such mechanism in NetHack.

So is The Eye.

So is Magicbane.

So is an off-hand silver saber matched to
Greyswander or Mjollnir.

So are lenses.

You're arguing for fixing something that is
Profoundly Not Broken.

You won't find hordes of _new_ NetHack players
agitating for taking out all the middle game stuff
that rids the game of early survival crisis issues
(only to find them replaced with later survival
crisis issues).

These railings against game features that make the
game "too easy" seem to come purely from those
who've 1) learned to beat the game, and usually 2)
gotten spoiled rotten with winning the game as a
reason for self-spoilage [thus ruining most of the
game and all of its neat hidden features for
themselves, VOLUNTARILY, and I should feel sorry for
them that the game now seems boring to them, why
again, one more time?]

        [Notice for example that a unicorn horn has
        very limited use to the unspoiled player,
        until each feature in turn has been
        discovered. It is only the self-spoiled who
        find it "overpowerful".

        To the unspoiled it is instead a repeated
        source of "Hey!  Neat! I didn't know it
        would do that!"

        For example, until spoiled on rgrn, I had no
        idea that a unicorn horn could be used as an
        ascension weapon of choice by some roles.]

The need for the more experienced players' continued
enjoyment of the game isn't to break the game to
keep the NAO "horde of super-players" stats from
improving

        [Who cares about players with mutant ninja
        NetHacking skills getting so good they have
        no way left to earn bragging rights in
        comparison to one another because the field
        has leveled off at the high end of the
        playing skills distribution?

        That's like worrying about tax rates for the
        rich being too high.

        If you've really really worn out NetHack,
        instead of ruining it for others just
        starting out, merely to cater to you at the
        end of your NetHack playing career, FIND A
        NEW GAME TO PLAY!

        There's _nothing sacred_ about NetHack.

        There's no reason it _needs_ to endure
        forever or _needs_ to keep any one player
        playing it for a lifetime, or even keep any
        one player playing "past the first win"
        (other players do, and it is exactly the
        present misplaced focus on "ascension" as
        "all NetHack has to offer" that makes those
        few players bail once they've ascended).

        There's no reason to worry that it can be
        beaten by playing parts of it with a 'bot

                ["Rog-a-matic", which could win
                Rogue all by itself, anyone? The
                game of Rogue stayed completely
                playable despite that a 'Bot could
                play it too, just as chess does
                today despite Deep Blue.]

        by those with no better thing to do with the
        game.

        There's no reason it needs to be anything
        more than a very addictive introduction to
        dungeon crawl games, one that focuses less
        effort on a glitzy interface, more on game
        play complexity.

        There's certainly no reason it should become
        more and more specialized to cater to a
        smaller and smaller cadre of really expert
        players, at the expense of all the rest.]

The need for change, if there is any such need at
all, is not to make the game ever harder; AD&D made
that mistake. It is to extend the game to add new and
interesting features without it becoming a Slash-Em
clone.

Nor should it be necessary to do an AD&D "endless
arms race spiral" of ever more powerful monsters,
and ever more powerful artifacts with which to
battle them.

        I gave up on AD&D when the manual kit got to
        be too heavy for me, as a Dungeon Master, to
        cart around.

Instead, put in more "side-track" stuff like Ludius,
the Quest, the Planes, Sokoban, that provide
alternative strategies for winning the game.

Add new roles, new races, and more fully
differentiate the existing ones.

[Being unable by any means to acquire a different
role's quest artifact would be a simple change that
would go a long way in that direction.]

There's no real limit to the bushiness of the
NetHack DoD tree, no real limit to the "peachy keen"
features that can be added, but seeing all the
hidden stuff turn into instant spoilers must be
really demotivating for the Dev Team.

Quantum valeat.

